Executive Summary

The Institute has identified a substantial increase in reported fighting across democratic political systems.

Initial observations suggested a national emergency.

Politicians were fighting for communities.

Fighting for taxpayers.

Fighting for freedom.

Fighting the establishment.

Fighting bureaucrats.

Fighting elites.

Fighting legislation.

Fighting investigations.

Fighting other politicians who were themselves simultaneously fighting.

At several points it appeared that substantial portions of Westminster, Washington and various conference centres had become active combat zones.

Further investigation found remarkably few ambulances.

No artillery was located.

No cavalry units were observed.

Most combatants were wearing suits.

Several were holding microphones.

One had apparently been “fighting all morning” despite having spent the relevant period sitting in a television studio.

The Institute has therefore provisionally classified the phenomenon as:

BVS — Borrowed Violence Syndrome

A rhetorical condition in which an individual acquires the language, symbolism and prestige of physical struggle without encountering the physical component normally associated with it.

A secondary condition has also been identified:

BCS — Borrowed Courage Syndrome

This occurs when the speaker receives some of the heroic status normally associated with taking genuine personal risks merely by describing routine political activity using verbs previously associated with soldiers, dissidents, boxers and animals attempting not to be eaten.

The Institute stresses that political disagreement remains permitted.

Indeed, this appears to be the entire point of democracy.

