5. Political Language Before the Discovery of the Microphone

The Institute must emphasise that political fighting was not always theatrical.

Historically, political activists sometimes encountered an inconvenient phenomenon known as:

actual consequences.

American civil-rights campaigners participating in peaceful marches could be beaten, arrested or attacked.

People attempting to register to vote could face violence.

Freedom Riders were physically assaulted.

Political dissidents under authoritarian systems could lose employment, liberty or life.

Polish Solidarity activists operated under surveillance, repression and eventually martial law.

British suffrage campaigners were arrested and imprisoned; some endured force-feeding.

In these environments, the statement:

“We are fighting for our rights”

possessed considerably more physical content.

The marcher might not strike anybody.

Somebody might nevertheless strike the marcher.

This distinction has apparently been lost somewhere between Birmingham, Alabama and the modern conference buffet.

⸻

6. Discovery of Borrowed Courage

Political vocabulary therefore contains inherited value.

Words such as:

fight

struggle

resistance

defiance

sacrifice

acquired some of their moral seriousness from people for whom those words involved genuine personal danger.

Modern politics inherited the vocabulary.

It did not necessarily inherit the danger.

Researchers describe this as Borrowed Courage.

The mechanism is remarkably efficient.

Consider:

“I disagree with the government’s proposed regulatory framework.”

The speaker appears to be a politician.

Now consider:

“I am fighting the establishment.”

The speaker has become Che Guevara somewhere between sentences despite remaining in the same chair.

No additional physical risk is required.

The transformation is entirely lexical.

