9. The Dana White Problem

At this stage the Institute encountered an observation from an unexpected research discipline:

combat-sports promotion.

Dana White has repeatedly made a simple point about human attention.

Put various sporting activities around a public space and then allow a fight to break out.

People look at the fight.

The precise formulation has circulated in several forms, including the well-known street-corner analogy associated with boxing commentator Max Kellerman and repeated in combat-sports discussion.

The underlying observation is brutally simple:

fights attract attention.

For a UFC promoter, this is useful commercial information.

For political communication, it is potentially catastrophic.

Because the politician does not actually need a fight.

They need only persuade the audience that one exists.

