10. Conflict as Attention Technology

Consider two political statements.

Statement A

“The opposition has raised objections to Clause 14.”

Statement B

“They’re attacking everything we stand for and we’re going to fight back.”

Researchers presented both statements to the imaginary Department of Human Attention.

Statement A was immediately transferred to BBC Parliament at 2:13 a.m.

Statement B generated six television panels, fourteen clips, three fundraising emails and a man on social media demanding revolution before lunch.

The underlying legislative event remained unchanged.

Only its rhetorical packaging changed.

This suggests that conflict operates as an attention technology.

A fight naturally contains:

* danger;
* uncertainty;
* sides;
* escalation;
* winners;
* losers;
* anticipation.

Politics packaged as a fight inherits those properties.

It therefore becomes easier to watch.

