13. Rhetorical Conscription

The most concerning phenomenon is not that politicians call themselves fighters.

It is that audiences are gradually recruited into the imaginary battle.

The sequence normally resembles:

They’re attacking us.

They hate people like you.

The system is rigged against us.

They are destroying the country.

We cannot surrender.

We have to fight.

Keep fighting.

No explicit instruction to commit violence is necessary.

Indeed, explicit instructions would be legally inconvenient.

Instead, the worldview is constructed first.

This process is classified as:

Rhetorical Conscription

The citizen has not been given a weapon.

They have been given something potentially more important:

an enemy.

