15. The Metaphor Escape Problem

Political actors often defend martial language on the basis that:

“Obviously I didn’t mean literally fight.”

This reveals an unusual model of communication in which the speaker apparently controls the brain of everybody listening.

Researchers have been unable to reproduce this effect.

When words are transmitted to one million people, approximately one million separate nervous systems interpret them.

Most correctly understand metaphor.

Some are angry.

Some are frightened.

Some already believe civilisation is collapsing.

Some believe institutions are illegitimate.

Some have spent six hours watching videos explaining that their neighbours are traitors.

The speaker cannot determine which listener receives the statement as:

metaphorical encouragement to participate democratically

and which receives it as:

finally somebody understands what must be done.

The Institute therefore proposes the Metaphor Escape Principle:

The speaker controls the release of the metaphor, not its final destination.

