16. The Zebra Test

To determine whether a claimed political fight possesses adequate reality, researchers developed the:

ZEBRA TEST

Procedure:

1. Locate subject claiming to be fighting for survival.
2. Compare subject’s circumstances with a zebra currently located in the jaws of a lion.
3. Observe differences.

Zebra:

* predator attached;
* immediate physical danger;
* elevated heart rate;
* possible death;
* no speechwriter.

Politician:

* microphone attached;
* security nearby;
* bottled water available;
* television booking at 7:30;
* possible book deal.

The Institute considers these environments operationally distinct.

⸻

17. The Zebra Requires No Messaging Strategy

The Zebra Test reveals a deeper principle.

The zebra never has to persuade observers that the situation is serious.

There is no:

ZEBRA CAMPAIGN HQ STATEMENT

“Today’s completely unacceptable lion attack demonstrates why we need your support now more than ever.”

No email arrives saying:

THE LION THOUGHT WE WOULD SURRENDER

Donate £10 before midnight.

No social-media manager posts:

🚨 BREAKING: LION TERRIFIED OF OUR MOVEMENT 🚨

The lion itself provides sufficient evidence.

This produces an important distinction:

Real danger generates attention naturally.

Rhetorical danger requires continuous narration.

