19. Actual Violence Remains Annoyingly Physical

The romanticisation of fighting encounters another difficulty:

real violence is unpleasant.

This apparently came as a surprise to several historical societies.

Violence involves:

pain;

fear;

broken bones;

permanent injury;

dead people;

people who did not intend to kill anybody discovering that they have;

people who expected glorious victory discovering intensive care.

It is chaotic.

Once initiated it becomes remarkably uninterested in the original rhetorical objectives.

A politician can clarify a statement the following morning.

A fractured skull does not usually accept clarifications.

