20. Why Democracy Was Invented

Researchers subsequently examined several political institutions and made an extraordinary discovery.

Courts.

Elections.

Parliaments.

Negotiation.

Law.

Due process.

All appear to share a common historical purpose:

preventing disagreements from becoming fights.

This creates a conceptual difficulty.

A politician can spend the morning operating inside institutions specifically created as alternatives to violence and spend the afternoon announcing that they are engaged in a war.

The Institute has labelled this:

Civilisational Amnesia.

Political debate is not failed combat.

It is successful non-combat.

That is supposed to be the achievement.

