22. Final Classification

The Institute therefore establishes the following terminology.

Borrowed Violence

Importing the emotional properties of physical conflict into situations containing little comparable physical danger.

Borrowed Courage

Receiving some of the prestige associated with genuine sacrifice through martial language.

The Safe Fighter

A political actor engaged in continuous rhetorical combat under unusually comprehensive security arrangements.

Rhetorical Conscription

Recruiting supporters into an imagined conflict by repeatedly supplying enemies, attacks, betrayal and existential threat.

Rhetorical Escalation by Selection

The tendency of attention-driven systems to reward increasingly dramatic descriptions of otherwise ordinary political events.

Conflict as Attention Technology

The use of real or manufactured confrontation to obtain attention that procedure would never receive.

The Zebra Test

A diagnostic procedure for determining whether the subject is actually fighting for survival or simply due on Good Morning Britain.

