Conclusion

There remain people in the world for whom the word fight requires no quotation marks.

Boxers know.

Soldiers know.

People attacked in the street know.

Political dissidents imprisoned by authoritarian governments know.

Parents protecting children know.

Human beings facing genuine physical danger know.

And somewhere on the African savannah, the zebra knows extremely well.

The remarkable feature of genuine fighting is that nobody involved needs to repeatedly announce that it is occurring.

Real danger contains its own evidence.

Political rhetoric frequently works in reverse.

The politician must continually remind the audience:

there is an attack;

there is an enemy;

there is a conspiracy;

there is a battle;

there can be no surrender;

the fight must continue.

And because fights attract attention, the media system looks.

Because the media system looks, the politician receives attention.

Because the politician receives attention, the language becomes more combative.

The imagined fight therefore becomes politically valuable precisely because the real fight is absent.

Which leaves the Institute with one final observation.

When Dana White says people look when a fight breaks out, he is describing an ancient feature of human attention.

When a zebra is in a lion’s jaws, it demonstrates why.

But when a well-protected politician stands behind a microphone announcing for the seventeenth time that afternoon that he is “fighting for his life,” the sensible response may simply be to inspect the room.

No lion.

No boxing gloves.

No incoming artillery.

No blood.

Just cameras.

And perhaps that was the fight all along:

the fight for your attention.
